---
import BlogLayout from "../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro";

const title = "New vs Old Tax Regime FY 2025-26: Slabs, 75k Deduction & Break-Even";
const description = "Comprehensive comparison between the New and Old Tax Regime for FY 2025-26 (AY 2026-27). Learn revised Section 115BAC slabs, ₹75,000 standard deduction, 87A rebate, and exact break-even thresholds.";
const image = "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1554224154-26032ffc0d07?w=1200&q=80";
const canonical = "https://finsiva.com/blogs/india-new-vs-old-tax-regime-fy-2025-26-guide/";

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What are the revised tax slabs under the New Tax Regime for FY 2025-26?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Under the New Tax Regime (Section 115BAC), taxable income up to ₹3,00,000 is taxed at 0%. Income between ₹3,00,001 and ₹7,00,000 is taxed at 5%. Income between ₹7,00,001 and ₹10,00,000 is taxed at 10%. Income between ₹10,00,001 and ₹12,00,000 is taxed at 15%. Income between ₹12,00,001 and ₹15,00,000 is taxed at 20%, and income exceeding ₹15,00,000 is taxed at the maximum rate of 30%."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How does the ₹75,000 standard deduction and Section 87A rebate make income up to ₹7.75 Lakhs tax-free?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Salaried taxpayers under the New Tax Regime receive an enhanced standard deduction of ₹75,000 (increased from ₹50,000 in Budget reforms). If your gross annual salary is ₹7,75,000, subtracting the ₹75,000 standard deduction reduces your net taxable income to ₹7,00,000. Under Section 87A, any resident individual with taxable income not exceeding ₹7,00,000 receives a full tax rebate of up to ₹25,000, resulting in zero net tax liability."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the break-even deduction point between the Old and New Tax Regime?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "The break-even deduction threshold is the total amount of tax deductions (80C, 80D, HRA, home loan interest) needed under the Old Regime to match the lower taxes of the New Regime. For a gross salary of ₹10 Lakhs, you need at least ₹2,50,000 in total deductions to make the Old Regime advantageous. For a gross salary of ₹15 Lakhs and above, you generally need between ₹3,75,000 and ₹4,25,000 in aggregate deductions to beat the New Regime."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can self-employed professionals switch between the New and Old Tax Regime annually?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Salaried individuals without business income can freely switch between the Old and New Tax Regimes every year when filing their Income Tax Return (ITR-1 or ITR-2). However, individuals with business or professional income (ITR-3 or ITR-4) can only switch back to the Old Regime once in their lifetime by filing Form 10-IEA before the due date, and cannot switch back to the New Regime unless they cease to have business income."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Are Section 80C and HRA deductions allowed under the New Tax Regime?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. Major personal tax exemptions and deductions—including Section 80C (PPF, ELSS, EPF, Life Insurance up to ₹1.5 Lakhs), Section 80D (Mediclaim), House Rent Allowance (HRA), Leave Travel Concession (LTC), and Section 24(b) home loan interest on self-occupied property (up to ₹2 Lakhs)—are strictly prohibited under the New Tax Regime."
      }
    }
  ]
};
---

<BlogLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  image={image}
  canonical={canonical}
  author="Vikramaditya Sharma, CA"
  authorBio="Vikramaditya Sharma is a Chartered Accountant and direct tax advisor based in Mumbai, specializing in personal financial planning, corporate tax compliance, and CBDT regulatory assessments."
  lastUpdated="October 2026"
  readTime="15 min read"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <div class="mt-8 space-y-6 text-body dark:text-muted leading-relaxed">
    <p>The annual debate between the <strong>New Tax Regime</strong> (Section 115BAC) and the traditional <strong>Old Tax Regime</strong> represents one of the most significant financial decisions for Indian taxpayers. Following successive Union Budget enhancements by the Ministry of Finance, the New Tax Regime has been cemented as the default tax system, featuring wider slabs, reduced marginal rates, an enhanced <strong>₹75,000 standard deduction</strong>, and comprehensive tax rebates under Section 87A.</p>

    <p>However, despite the aggressive government incentives pushing taxpayers toward the simplified system, the Old Tax Regime remains a powerful wealth-preservation tool for individuals carrying substantial tax-deductible commitments—including home loan interest, House Rent Allowance (HRA), National Pension System (NPS) contributions, and health insurance premiums. Deciding which path yields maximum take-home salary requires a precise mathematical evaluation of your personal deduction portfolio.</p>

    <!-- Quick Answer Card -->
    <div class="my-6 rounded-2xl border-2 border-primary/20 bg-primary/[0.03] p-6 dark:border-primary/30 dark:bg-primary/[0.05]">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
        <span class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-primary inline-block"></span>
        FY 2025-26 Tax Regime Rule of Thumb
      </h3>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-body dark:text-muted">
        <strong>Salaried income up to ₹7,75,000 is 100% tax-free under the New Tax Regime</strong> after factoring in the ₹75,000 standard deduction and the Section 87A rebate. If your gross income exceeds ₹15 Lakhs, you require at least <strong>₹3.75 Lakhs to ₹4.25 Lakhs in verifiable deductions</strong> (80C, 80D, HRA, home loan interest) for the Old Regime to yield lower taxes than the New Regime.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-8">What Are the Revised Slabs Under the New Tax Regime (Section 115BAC)?</h2>
    <p><strong>Under the New Tax Regime, taxable income is structured into six simplified progressive brackets, starting at 0% for income up to ₹3,00,000 and topping out at 30% for income exceeding ₹15,00,000.</strong> By compressing rate jumps and eliminating almost all itemized deductions, the Income Tax Department aims to minimize administrative scrutiny while lowering effective tax rates across the middle class.</p>

    <div class="my-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-canvas-soft border-b border-hairline dark:bg-surface/80 dark:border-overlay-lighter text-ink dark:text-on-primary font-bold">
          <tr>
            <th class="p-3">Taxable Income Slab (INR)</th>
            <th class="p-3">New Regime Tax Rate</th>
            <th class="p-3">Old Regime Tax Rate</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-hairline dark:divide-overlay-lighter">
          <tr class="bg-primary/[0.02]"><td class="p-3 font-semibold">₹0 – ₹2,50,000</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-primary dark:text-on-primary">Nil (0%)</td><td class="p-3">Nil (0%)</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">₹2,50,001 – ₹3,00,000</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-primary dark:text-on-primary">Nil (0%)</td><td class="p-3">5%</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-3">₹3,00,001 – ₹5,00,000</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-primary dark:text-on-primary">5%</td><td class="p-3">5%</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-3">₹5,00,001 – ₹7,00,000</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-primary dark:text-on-primary">5%</td><td class="p-3">20%</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-3">₹7,00,001 – ₹10,00,000</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-primary dark:text-on-primary">10%</td><td class="p-3">20%</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-3">₹10,00,001 – ₹12,00,000</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-primary dark:text-on-primary">15%</td><td class="p-3">30%</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-3">₹12,00,001 – ₹15,00,000</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-primary dark:text-on-primary">20%</td><td class="p-3">30%</td></tr>
          <tr class="bg-canvas-soft"><td class="p-3 font-semibold">Above ₹15,00,000</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-primary dark:text-on-primary">30%</td><td class="p-3">30%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="text-xs text-muted">
      *Note: A 4% Health and Education Cess is levied on total income tax across both regimes. In the Old Regime, the basic exemption limit is ₹3,00,000 for senior citizens (age 60 to 79) and ₹5,00,000 for super senior citizens (age 80+).
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-8">How Does the ₹7.75 Lakh Zero-Tax Benefit Work?</h2>
    <p><strong>Salaried employees earning up to ₹7,75,000 gross annual compensation pay exactly zero income tax under the New Tax Regime through the combination of the ₹75,000 standard deduction and the Section 87A full tax rebate.</strong></p>

    <p>Here is the exact step-by-step breakdown:</p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-1.5 mt-2 text-sm">
      <li><strong>Gross Salaried Income:</strong> ₹7,75,000</li>
      <li><strong>Less Standard Deduction:</strong> −₹75,000 (Available automatically to all salaried and pensioned taxpayers)</li>
      <li><strong>Net Taxable Income:</strong> ₹7,00,000</li>
      <li><strong>Computed Tax on ₹7,00,000:</strong>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 mt-1 space-y-0.5">
          <li>₹0 to ₹3,00,000 @ 0% = ₹0</li>
          <li>₹3,00,001 to ₹7,00,000 (₹4,00,000 @ 5%) = ₹20,000</li>
          <li>Total Base Tax = ₹20,000</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Section 87A Tax Rebate:</strong> Full rebate of ₹20,000 (Section 87A covers up to ₹25,000 tax for resident individuals earning up to ₹7 Lakhs net taxable income).</li>
      <li><strong>Final Tax Payable:</strong> <strong>₹0 (Zero)</strong>.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-8">Which Deductions Are Allowed vs Prohibited Under the New Regime?</h2>
    <p><strong>The New Tax Regime disallows over 70 popular exemptions and deductions, including Section 80C, Section 80D, and HRA, while retaining employer NPS contributions under Section 80CCD(2), the standard deduction on salary and family pension, and conveyance allowances for official duties.</strong></p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-6">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas p-5 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface shadow-sm">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary text-base text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400 mb-2">Permitted Under New Regime</h3>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1.5 text-sm">
          <li><strong>Standard Deduction:</strong> ₹75,000 on salary income (or ₹25,000 on family pension).</li>
          <li><strong>Employer NPS Contribution:</strong> Section 80CCD(2) up to 14% of Basic + DA for government employees, and 10% (expanded to 14% for corporate employees in recent amendments).</li>
          <li><strong>Agniveer Corpus Fund:</strong> Deductions under Section 80CCH.</li>
          <li><strong>Official Allowances:</strong> Travelling allowance, daily allowance for official tours, and transport allowance for specially-abled employees.</li>
          <li><strong>Voluntary Retirement Scheme (VRS):</strong> Exemption up to ₹5,00,000 under Section 10(10C).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas p-5 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface shadow-sm">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary text-base text-red-600 dark:text-red-400 mb-2">Disallowed Under New Regime</h3>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1.5 text-sm">
          <li><strong>Section 80C:</strong> Up to ₹1,50,000 (EPF, PPF, ELSS, Life Insurance, Sukanya Samriddhi, Home Loan Principal).</li>
          <li><strong>Section 80D:</strong> Mediclaim health insurance premiums (up to ₹25,000 for self/family, ₹50,000 for parents).</li>
          <li><strong>House Rent Allowance (HRA):</strong> Section 10(13A) rental exemption.</li>
          <li><strong>Section 24(b):</strong> Home loan interest deduction up to ₹2,00,000 on self-occupied residential property.</li>
          <li><strong>Leave Travel Concession (LTC):</strong> Section 10(5) travel exemptions.</li>
          <li><strong>Section 80TTA / 80TTB:</strong> Savings account interest deductions.</li>
          <li><strong>Section 80E:</strong> Education loan interest deductions.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-8">The Break-Even Deduction Table: When Does the Old Regime Win?</h2>
    <p><strong>The break-even point is the exact total deduction amount at which the tax liability under both the Old and New Regimes is identical; if your eligible deductions exceed this threshold, the Old Regime saves you more money.</strong></p>

    <div class="my-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-canvas-soft border-b border-hairline dark:bg-surface/80 dark:border-overlay-lighter text-ink dark:text-on-primary font-bold">
          <tr>
            <th class="p-3">Gross Salaried Income (INR)</th>
            <th class="p-3">New Regime Tax (₹75k Std Ded)</th>
            <th class="p-3">Break-Even Deductions Required</th>
            <th class="p-3">Recommended Regime</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-hairline dark:divide-overlay-lighter">
          <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">₹7,50,000</td><td class="p-3">₹0</td><td class="p-3">₹2,50,000</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-primary dark:text-on-primary">New Regime (No investment stress)</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">₹10,00,000</td><td class="p-3">₹54,600</td><td class="p-3">₹2,62,500</td><td class="p-3">Old Regime if HRA + 80C > ₹2.63L</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">₹12,50,000</td><td class="p-3">₹85,800</td><td class="p-3">₹3,12,500</td><td class="p-3">Old Regime if Home Loan + 80C + 80D</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">₹15,00,000</td><td class="p-3">₹1,30,000</td><td class="p-3">₹3,58,333</td><td class="p-3">Old Regime if Deductions > ₹3.58L</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">₹20,00,000</td><td class="p-3">₹2,73,000</td><td class="p-3">₹3,75,000</td><td class="p-3">Old Regime if Deductions > ₹3.75L</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">₹25,00,000</td><td class="p-3">₹4,29,000</td><td class="p-3">₹4,25,000</td><td class="p-3">New Regime for most earners</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="text-sm">
      <strong>Real-World Case Study:</strong> A tech professional earning ₹18,00,000 with a home loan paying ₹2,00,000 annual interest (Section 24b), ₹1,50,000 in EPF/ELSS (Section 80C), ₹50,000 health insurance for senior parents (Section 80D), and the ₹50,000 standard deduction accumulates ₹4,50,000 in total Old Regime deductions. In this scenario, the Old Regime results in a tax of ₹2,02,800, compared to ₹2,10,600 under the New Regime, delivering a net saving of <strong>₹7,800</strong> under the Old Regime.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-8">Switching Rules: How Salaried Employees vs Business Owners Switch Regimes</h2>
    <p><strong>Salaried employees can switch between the Old and New Tax Regimes freely every single financial year when filing their Income Tax Return, whereas business owners and professionals can only opt out of the New Regime once in their lifetime.</strong></p>

    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-6">Rules for Salaried Individuals (ITR-1 / ITR-2):</h3>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1.5 mt-2 text-sm">
      <li>You can declare your preferred regime to your employer at the beginning of the financial year for monthly TDS calculations.</li>
      <li>Even if your employer deducted tax under the New Regime during the year, you are legally entitled to switch to the Old Regime (or vice versa) when filing your annual ITR on the Income Tax e-filing portal before July 31.</li>
      <li>No separate form is required; you simply select your choice on the ITR utility.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-4">Rules for Business & Profession Income (ITR-3 / ITR-4):</h3>
    <p class="text-sm">
      Individuals deriving income from a business or profession must file <strong>Form 10-IEA</strong> on or before the due date under Section 139(1) to opt out of the default New Tax Regime. Once they switch back to the Old Regime, they are granted only one opportunity in their lifetime to return to the New Regime.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-12 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
  {faqSchema.mainEntity.map(faq => (
